 "narrative": {
  "northStar": "The Bench Instrument",
  "overview": "A well-made meter on a workbench. Readings line up, nothing decorates, and a fault is obvious. The interface is plain and exact, calm, and friendly in the way a good tool is: short words that help, with no chatter.\n\nEverything sits on one faint grey-green bench. One teal, the colour of the coating on a circuit board, marks what can be operated and what is current. Green, amber and red belong to state and nothing else, so when one appears it means something.\n\nIt must never look like the vendor's old web UI (frames and grey forms), and it must never look like a marketing dashboard (big number tiles, gradients, charts for show).",
  "keyCharacteristics": [
   "System type with tabular figures; numbers align and hold still.",
   "Light and dark follow the viewer's setting, with the same roles in both.",
   "Tables for readings, plain forms for settings, one column of controls at a time.",
   "Colour and motion only when something is operable, current, or wrong."
  ],
  "rules": [
   {
    "name": "The State-Only Rule",
    "body": "Green, amber and red appear only for state, never as decoration or category.",
    "section": "colors"
   },
   {
    "name": "The Dot-and-Word Rule",
    "body": "State is never colour alone: a dot and a word carry it together.",
    "section": "colors"
   },
   {
    "name": "The Steady Figures Rule",
